Real MUI programs on WinUAE
===========================

programs.tsv lists 182 freely downloadable MUI programs, taken over from
OpenMUI's real-program suite: group mui3 (must run on MUI 3.8), mui5
(needs or wants MUI 4/5) and mcc (the demos of add-on classes). Columns:
id, group, key (the archive's directory in the cache), archive (Aminet
path, or a URL for the few that are elsewhere), dir (the program's
directory inside the unpacked archive), exe (relative to dir, with
arguments), wait (seconds to wait for its first window), title (pattern of
its window title, where the window belongs to another process than the
command) and notes. support.tsv lists the third-party classes and
libraries the programs use, with the archive and path each comes from.

Fetching
--------

  tests/real/fetch.py [--group mui3] [--ids ID,...] [--cache DIR] [--list]

downloads the archives of the chosen programs and of support.tsv from
Aminet into ~/.cache/zune68-real/<key>/ and unpacks each into a new
directory <key>/x with lha. They are third-party software: never commit or
redistribute them. Nothing in them is run on the host. Archives that are
not on Aminet (YAM 2.5 and 2.10, SimpleMail, IBrowse, MUIbase, MUI 5,
UP-MCC) are reported as "not on Aminet"; put such an archive into
<key>/ by hand and run fetch.py again to unpack it.

Running
-------

  make -j8 core plugins prefs                  (for the zune target)
  tests/real/run.py --fixture FIX --target zune  [--group mui3] [--first N]
  tests/real/run.py --fixture FIX --target mui38 [--ids ID,...] [--repeat N]

FIX is a WinUAE fixture directory: run.uae mounts FIX/work as Work: and
boots an OS 3.1 system whose Startup-Sequence ends with "Execute
Work:stage"; for mui38 that system has MUI 3.8 installed and on LIBS:.
The emulator is /Applications/WinUAE.app/Contents/MacOS/WinUAE, or
--emulator / $WINUAE. Only the emulator started with FIX/run.uae is ever
stopped, and a running one is stopped before booting it again.

On the zune target Zune68's build (muimaster.library and Libs/MUI, from
--lib, default build/muimaster) comes first on LIBS: and MUI:. On both
targets the classes and libraries of support.tsv come last on LIBS:, so
a class that MUI 3.8 or Zune68 brings is used in place of the archive's.

Each boot runs up to --batch programs (default 15) with the guest helper
wintool (wintool.c). For each program it records the free memory, starts
the program in its directory, waits for a window that was not there
before, lets the windows settle, saves the screen, asks the program to
quit and records the free memory again, with the libraries and classes
that failed to open meanwhile. Quitting goes step by step: Ctrl-C to its
processes, IDCMP_CLOSEWINDOW to each of its windows, then answering
requesters (Amiga-V on a system requester, Return on a window that
opened since), the pick of a menu item named Quit, Exit or Beenden,
closing again, and at last answering every window of it (Amiga-B on a
system requester). A start-up requester counts as the first window, and
is answered only in that last step. A program that
does not quit ends the boot, and the rest go on in a new one; so do the
remaining programs after a guest stops making progress (a crash or a
hang, --stall seconds beyond the program's wait) or resets. A boot that
runs no program is tried again; after three such boots in a row the run
stops and writes what it has.

Results go to build/real/<target>/ (or --out):

  results.tsv   id, started, seconds to the first window, free memory
                before, with the windows open and after, delta (after
                minus before), quit (ctrlc, close, answer, menu), status
                (ok, nowindow, exited: ended without a window, stuck: did
                not quit, hang, reset, not fetched), the windows (x,y wxh
                title), the libraries/classes that failed to open, and
                free chip memory, largest free block and largest free
                chip block before and after
  <id>.png      the screen of its first window, with its windows open
  <id>.con      what it printed
  <id>.log      wintool's lines for it

The first program of a boot also pays for loading muimaster.library and
its classes, so its delta is large; memory is never flushed in between.
--repeat N runs each program N times in a row in one boot (ids <id>,
<id>.2, ...): from the second run on everything it loads is there
already, so what its delta still shows is a leak.
